      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Many critics argue that sweeping international trade agreements diminish national sovereignty by binding countries to global rules they can't easily break. These policies are often perceived as prioritizing multinational corporate interests over local needs, forcing domestic industries to compete on a global stage. Consequently, national governments sometimes find their ability to protect their own workers or steer economic development according to domestic priorities severely constrained. This creates a widespread sentiment that globalist economic trends undermine the power of the individual nation-state.",
      "source": "target",
