    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so if it's basically inevitable that these deals lead to a single global paradigm, isn't it kind of a lost cause for anyone who actually wants to prioritize their own country's sovereignty? like, is \"national interest\" even a real thing anymore in this system, or is it just an illusion at this point?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "From a strict interpretation of global integration, the notion of absolute national economic sovereignty may indeed feel diminished. However, \"national interest\" is evolving, shifting from complete isolation to strategic navigation within the global system. Countries can still exert influence through targeted negotiations, strategic partnerships, and domestic policy adjustments to protect vital sectors. The debate, then, becomes less about *absolute* sovereignty and more about effectively wielding *relative* power within the existing interconnected framework.",
      "source": "target",
